Subject: [PATCH] holy-grail: take bdisp from the pattern-matched lines

HolyGrail.solve_patterns set patt_dict['bdisp'] to the dispersion at the
peak of the pattern histogram.  For quadrangle patterns that value is
truncated to an integer (patterns.quadrangles stores dispersions in
np.ulonglong arrays), e.g. 1.94 A/pix -> 1.  bdisp is passed by
wv_fitting.fit_slit to iterative_fitting, which uses it to convert the fit
rms to pixels and the line-matching tolerance to Angstroms, so the stored
rms, the rms_thresh test, and match_toler were wrong by the same factor
(about 2x on Subaru/MOIRCS VB_K, 1.94 A/pix).

solve_patterns now takes bdisp from a linear fit to the lines matched by
the patterns (at least two), falling back to the histogram value.  Only
holy-grail is affected: fit_slit and iterative_fitting are unchanged, so
reidentify, full_template and echelle calibrations are not.  cross_match
already takes its dispersion from good solutions.

The quadrangles dtype itself is not changed here: on the MOIRCS VB_K test
mask, making it float changes 13 of 15 affected solutions by 29-112 A to
worse solutions.  See the bug report in pypeitdev/subaru_moircs_vbk.

Verified on the MOIRCS VB_K test mask (28 slits): same result as the
fix-B test in holy_rms_check.py; stored rms = recomputed rms; no
solution changes by more than 0.14 A; 0-2 more lines on affected slits.
Not yet run through the development suite.

New test: pypeit/tests/test_holygrail_dispersion.py (fails on the
current code for the quadrangle cases).

---
--- a/pypeit/core/wavecal/autoid.py
+++ b/pypeit/core/wavecal/autoid.py
@@ -2959,6 +2959,16 @@
         patt_dict['sign'] = sign
         patt_dict['bwv'] = wcen
         patt_dict['bdisp'] = 10.0 ** dcen
+        # The histogram estimate above can be far off: patterns.quadrangles
+        # stores pattern dispersions as unsigned integers, so e.g. 1.94 A/pix
+        # becomes 1.  bdisp is used by wv_fitting.iterative_fitting to
+        # convert the fit rms and the line-matching tolerance between pixels
+        # and Angstroms, so take it from the pattern-matched lines instead.
+        ok = np.where(patt_dict['mask'])[0]
+        if ok.size >= 2:
+            ids = np.asarray(patt_dict['IDs'], dtype=float)[ok]
+            slope = np.polyfit(use_tcent[ok], ids, 1)[0]
+            patt_dict['bdisp'] = float(np.abs(slope))
 
         # Check that a solution has been found
         if patt_dict['nmatch'] == 0 and self._verbose:
--- /dev/null
+++ b/pypeit/tests/test_holygrail_dispersion.py
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+"""
+Tests for the dispersion estimate that holy-grail passes to the
+wavelength fit.
+"""
+import numpy as np
+import pytest
+
+from pypeit.core.wavecal import autoid, patterns
+from pypeit.par import pypeitpar
+
+# Twelve lines with irregular spacing on a linear wavelength solution
+TCENT = np.array([10., 31., 55., 92., 140., 171., 230., 266., 333., 370.,
+                  425., 490.])
+NPIX = 2048
+
+
+def _holygrail_stub(wave):
+    """
+    A HolyGrail instance with only the attributes solve_patterns uses, so
+    that the brute-force search in __init__ does not run.
+    """
+    hg = autoid.HolyGrail.__new__(autoid.HolyGrail)
+    hg._par = pypeitpar.WavelengthSolutionPar()
+    hg._wvdata = wave
+    hg._npix = NPIX
+    hg._verbose = False
+    return hg
+
+
+@pytest.mark.parametrize('disp_true', [0.5, 1.94, 3.3])
+def test_quadrangle_bdisp(disp_true):
+    # Patterns as holy-grail builds them for poly=4
+    wave = 20000. + disp_true * (TCENT - TCENT[0])
+    dindex, lindex, wvcen, disps = patterns.quadrangles(
+        TCENT, wave, NPIX, detsrch=5, lstsrch=5, pixtol=1.0)
+    assert disps.size > 0
+    # The histogram peak that solve_slit would pass on: the dispersion of
+    # the first pattern (possibly truncated), and all pattern indices
+    dcen = np.log10(max(float(disps[0]), 10**-1.5))
+    bestlist = [float(wvcen[0]), dcen, 1, +1, dindex.flatten(),
+                lindex.flatten()]
+    hg = _holygrail_stub(wave)
+    patt = hg.solve_patterns(0, bestlist, [TCENT, np.zeros_like(TCENT)])
+    assert patt is not None
+    # bdisp comes from the matched lines, not from the histogram bin
+    assert np.isclose(patt['bdisp'], disp_true, rtol=1e-6)
+
+
+def test_triangle_bdisp_unchanged():
+    # Triangle patterns already carry the right dispersion
+    disp_true = 1.94
+    wave = 20000. + disp_true * (TCENT - TCENT[0])
+    dindex, lindex, wvcen, disps = patterns.triangles(
+        TCENT, wave, NPIX, detsrch=5, lstsrch=5, pixtol=1.0)
+    good = np.abs(disps / disp_true - 1) < 1e-6
+    bestlist = [float(np.median(wvcen[good])), np.log10(disp_true), 1, +1,
+                dindex[good].flatten(), lindex[good].flatten()]
+    hg = _holygrail_stub(wave)
+    patt = hg.solve_patterns(0, bestlist, [TCENT, np.zeros_like(TCENT)])
+    assert patt is not None
+    assert np.isclose(patt['bdisp'], disp_true, rtol=1e-6)
